# Render (and Netlify) read this file from the publish directory and apply
# these headers to matching responses. This is the actual fix for the
# "Failed to fetch dynamically imported module" / 404 on old asset hashes
# error after every redeploy:
#
#   Vite content-hashes every JS/CSS chunk filename (e.g.
#   SearchView-DB-LsWLY.js). Every time you deploy, filenames CHANGE if
#   their content changed. If a browser (or Render's own CDN) has index.html
#   cached from BEFORE the latest deploy, it still references the OLD
#   hashed filenames - which no longer exist on the server after the new
#   build replaced them. That's the literal 404 in the console.
#
# Fix: index.html (and any other non-hashed file) must never be cached, so
# every visit always fetches the index.html that matches the CURRENTLY
# deployed assets. The hashed files under /assets/ are safe to cache
# forever, since a changed file always gets a new filename - there's no
# such thing as a "stale" hashed asset, only a missing one.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
